WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:09.760
As a very general rule, there are three types&nbsp;
of information that are encoded in genomes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:14.720
Probably the one that most people are&nbsp;
familiar with are genes that encode proteins.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:21.200
But, in the human genome, this represents only&nbsp;
5%, or even a little less of the entire genome.&nbsp;

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:26.640
There are also regions, and nobody really&nbsp;
knows at present exactly where they all are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:32.160
and how big they are. But we know very well&nbsp;
they exist and we've identified many of them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:38.240
that control when genes are turned on or off and&nbsp;
how much. These are so-called regulatory regions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:44.320
There are 2 different parts of the regulator&nbsp;
regions. There are promoters that are located&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:48.800
just before a particular gene that regulate&nbsp;
gene transcription, and there are other more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:53.360
mysterious parts that are called enhancers,&nbsp;
located far away from the gene in question.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:59.520
It turns out that the enhancers, which we think of&nbsp;
as being very far away from the gene in question,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:05.920
actually may not be so far away. That's because&nbsp;
up to now, we've been studying DNA and genomics in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:13.280
two dimensions. But life is in three dimensions.
For a molecule of DNA, we can stretch it out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:17.200
measure it, and read it in the ways that&nbsp;
the modern technology allow us to do.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:22.800
But that's not how it works in the body.&nbsp;
That's not how it works inside the cell.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:28.640
The DNA molecules are coiled, and when you&nbsp;
look at three dimensional aspects of DNA,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:33.520
you sometimes find that these enhancers that&nbsp;
previously, mysteriously seemed to be very far&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:38.480
away from the gene they are regulating, are&nbsp;
actually quite close by. And that's one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:43.680
the really exciting new areas, studying DNA in&nbsp;
three dimensions, and when you include a time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:50.480
component to that, actually, four dimensions. So,&nbsp;
a whole new science of 4D genomics is taking shape&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:55.440
to understand the physical structure of a genome,&nbsp;
and the temporal aspects under which it functions.&nbsp;

00:01:56.960 --> 00:02:01.760
The third type of important element that&nbsp;
you find also in all organisms, are regions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:08.480
genes that encode RNA molecules. And RNA molecules&nbsp;
are also essential components of every cell.&nbsp;

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:13.280
Another part has to do with parts of&nbsp;
the genome that aren't regulatory,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:18.800
and that are not genes, and that are actually&nbsp;
relics of genes past. There's a lot of stretches&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:24.320
of the genome that don't currently or produce&nbsp;
any functional proteins. But yet it's still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:29.440
part of a long stretch and it still gets copied&nbsp;
and passed down from generation to generation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:35.360
A lot of this has been referred to as junk DNA,&nbsp;
and there's a bit of debate about whether it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:41.280
truly junk. One of the best explanations about&nbsp;
it was that, don't confuse junk with trash.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:48.160
Trash is useless, you throw away. Junk is what you&nbsp;
keep in your garage because it might one day be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:54.240
useful or used to be useful and then it broke but&nbsp;
it's still there so you keep it. You pass it on.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.120 --> 00:03:00.720
So, there's a difference between junk and trash.&nbsp;
We think that maybe large parts of the genome,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:04.880
which don't have what we think of as functional&nbsp;
genes in them, had been there because large&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:10.720
parts of the genome can get copied and put in a&nbsp;
different location. So, if we think about genes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:15.680
as reflecting the genome as a recipe book, as&nbsp;
an instruction book, there's a whole other side&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:20.560
to the genome and that is the genome as a history&nbsp;
book reflecting the evolutionary history of that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:26.880
particular species. This has been a marvelous area&nbsp;
of discovery leading to all sorts of insights,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:32.720
especially but not limited to our understanding&nbsp;
of human evolution. We can read the genome as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

